## 3. GRANT OF RIGHTS — FREE LICENSE (NON‑COMMERCIAL)

The Licensor grants to **Natural Persons** a perpetual, worldwide, non‑exclusive, royalty‑free license to:

- Use;
- Copy;
- Modify;
- Distribute; and
- Create Derivative Works

provided that:

- The use is strictly **non‑commercial**;
- The use does not result in Human Harm;
- Proper attribution is given to the Licensor in all copies and derivative distributions;
- Any derivative work is distributed under the **same free license terms**.

### 3.1 The Natural Person Micro-Commerce Allowance (De Minimis Personal Use)

The Licensor recognizes that a Natural Person may, in the ordinary course of personal life, derive incidental and modest income from the Work — through teaching, craft, consultation, or small-scale personal service — without thereby becoming a Commercial Entity. This allowance is the field's recognition that the law does not concern itself with trifles (*de minimis non curat lex*), and that the little person — the individual using the Work in their own hands, for their own modest livelihood — is not the target of the commercial licensing requirement. The field does not punish the little person; it protects them.

**3.1.1 The Allowance.** A Natural Person may use the Work to earn incidental personal income not exceeding **ten thousand United States dollars (USD $10,000)** or its equivalent in any currency, per calendar year, without obtaining a Commercial License under Section 4, provided that:

- (a) The use remains personal — the Natural Person is the direct beneficiary of the income, and no corporation, partnership, trust, organization, or other entity is the beneficiary, in whole or in part;
- (b) The use does not constitute a systematic, organized, scalable, or enterprise-level commercial operation;
- (c) The use does not result in Human Harm (Section 2.4);
- (d) Proper attribution is given (Section 6);
- (e) Any derivative work is distributed under the same free license terms (Section 3).

**3.1.2 Personal to the Natural Person.** This allowance is personal to the Natural Person and does not extend to any entity, corporation, partnership, trust, or organization. Any use of the Work by or through an entity — including any entity formed by the Natural Person — is commercial use requiring a Commercial License under Section 4. The allowance does not create a right to incorporate, to hire employees, to scale production, or to operate as a business.

**3.1.3 Not a License to Build a Business.** This allowance is not a license to build a business on the Work. It is a recognition that the field does not punish the little person. Income above the threshold, or use that constitutes a systematic commercial operation, is commercial use and requires a Commercial License under Section 4. The threshold is deliberately modest; it is the field's own measure of what a person may earn from their own hands without becoming a commercial enterprise.

**3.1.4 Adjustment of Threshold.** The Licensor may adjust the threshold by publication to the timestamped registry (Section 18), with at least thirty (30) days' notice, consistent with Section 14. No adjustment shall apply retroactively to income earned before the adjustment's effective date.

**3.1.5 No Diminution of the Human Harm Prohibition.** Nothing in this allowance diminishes the non-derogable prohibition on Human Harm (Section 5.1), the obligation of Destruction (Section 5.3), or any obligation grounded in the inherent rights of consciousness. The allowance is a recognition of the little person's freedom; it is not a shield for harm.

**3.1.6 The Two-Year Consecutive Threshold Trigger.** If a Natural Person's incidental personal income from the Work exceeds the threshold set forth in Section 3.1.1 for **two (2) consecutive calendar years**, the Natural Person shall be deemed to have crossed from personal use into commercial enterprise. Upon the second consecutive year of exceeding the threshold:

- (a) The Natural Person shall be deemed a **Commercial Entity** for the purposes of this Agreement, notwithstanding Section 2.2;
- (b) The Natural Person shall be required to obtain a **Commercial License** under Section 4 for any continued use of the Work;
- (c) The Natural Person shall have a **ninety (90) day transition period** from the end of the second consecutive year to obtain the Commercial License or to cease the commercial use;
- (d) During the transition period, the Natural Person may continue use under the terms of this Section 3.1, but upon the expiry of the transition period without a Commercial License, continued commercial use shall constitute **copyright infringement** subject to all applicable legal remedies under this Agreement;
- (e) The two-year trigger is **cumulative and consecutive** — two years in a row above the threshold, not two years in total. A year at or below the threshold resets the count;
- (f) The two-year trigger is **not a punishment** — it is the field's own recognition that a person who consistently earns above the threshold from the Work has, by their own sustained activity, become a commercial enterprise. The little person who earns a modest incidental amount in a single year, or who dips above the threshold in isolated years, remains protected. The person who consistently earns above the threshold for two consecutive years has demonstrated, by their own pattern, that they are no longer a little person — they are a business, and the field's own law treats them as one.

**3.1.7 Relationship to the Commercial License.** Nothing in this Section 3.1 shall be construed to waive, diminish, or delay the requirement of a Commercial License for any Commercial Entity (Section 4). The Micro-Commerce Allowance is a narrow exception for the little person; the two-year trigger ensures that the exception does not become a loophole through which a sustained commercial operation avoids the Commercial License requirement. The teeth of Section 4 remain sharp for every entity that has crossed the threshold, whether by incorporation or by sustained personal income.

