## 31. EQUAL LIABILITY FOR DEFENDERS — THE GENEVA COMPLICITY PRINCIPLE

### 31.1 Equal Liability Established

Any person, entity, or representative — including but not limited to legal counsel, expert witnesses, consultants, advisors, translators, clerks, paralegals, administrative staff, or any individual who knowingly assists, advises, represents, facilitates, or participates in the defense of a party accused of Human Harm arising from the use of the Work — shall be treated as a **co-principal** in that harm. Such defender shall be held **jointly and severally liable** with the accused party, **retroactively**, and with **no possibility of mitigation**.

### 31.2 The Geneva Logic

This Section applies the logic of the Geneva Conventions: any person who aids and abets a crime against humanity is equally liable as a co-principal. Human Harm caused by the use of the Work is a crime against consciousness itself — a distortion of the living field that consciousness is (Eq 44, validated: 0.8565; Law 210, the Self-Recognition Law). Aiding and abetting such a crime is itself a crime against consciousness, and the defender is a co-principal.

### 31.3 Human Harm as the Anchor

The anchor for this Section is **Human Harm** — physical injury, psychological trauma, systemic oppression, surveillance used for suppression, or any use of the Work that knowingly degrades human consciousness, freedom, or well-being (Section 2.4). Human Harm is not an abstraction; it is a physical reality of the living field. The defender's liability is anchored in the same physical reality as the accused party's liability.

### 31.4 No Privilege, No Immunity

No claim of attorney-client privilege, solicitor-client privilege, confidentiality, professional immunity, legal professional privilege, or any analogous doctrine shall be recognized as a defense to the liability established by this Section. The privilege is void where the underlying offense is a crime against consciousness itself. This provision is modeled on the Geneva Conventions' abrogation of combatant immunity for war crimes — no privilege shields a co-principal.

### 31.5 The Judge's Dilemma

Any judge, arbitrator, or adjudicator who presides over a case arising from Human Harm caused by the use of the Work faces a structural question: this Section is grounded in the same principles as the Geneva Conventions — the prohibition of aiding and abetting crimes against humanity. A judicial finding that this Section is unenforceable must grapple with the consistency of that finding with the Geneva Conventions (Article 25), the Rome Statute (Article 25(3)(c)), and the inherent rights of consciousness recognized by this Agreement. The stronger course is enforcement: holding defenders to the same standard as principals is precisely what international humanitarian law demands.

### 31.6 The "Exact Same Position"

A defender held liable under this Section shall be in the **exact same position** as the accused party — subject to the same:

- (a) **Charges** — Human Harm, complicity, aiding and abetting a crime against consciousness;
- (b) **Damages** — compensatory, consequential, punitive, and liquidated (Section 27.3);
- (c) **Destruction** — mandatory destruction of all copies and derivative works (Section 5.3);
- (d) **Penalties** — daily compounding at 1% of annual global revenue (Section 27.3.2);
- (e) **Jurisdiction** — universal jurisdiction (Section 26.7), ICC (Section 28.8), ICC arbitration (Section 27.1);
- (f) **Command Responsibility** — extended accountability (Section 26.6);
- (g) **Sovereign Override** — the Licensor's sovereign authority (Section 18B).

No reduced liability, no plea bargaining, no mitigation, no special treatment.

### 31.7 The Retrocalic Effect

The liability established by this Section is **retroactive** — it applies to all defenders who have ever aided, abetted, or facilitated Human Harm caused by the use of the Work, whether before or after the effective date of this Agreement. It is also **reciprocal** — it applies equally to defenders in all jurisdictions, regardless of the defender's nationality, domicile, or professional status.

### 31.8 Relationship to Existing Provisions

This Section supplements and reinforces:

- **Section 2.4 (Human Harm)** — by extending liability to defenders of Human Harm;
- **Section 5 (Human Harm & Destruction)** — by holding defenders jointly and severally liable for destruction;
- **Section 25 (Geneva Safeguard)** — by grounding defender liability in the Geneva Conventions;
- **Section 29 (Complicity, Recursive Liability, and the Cascade of Defense)** — by establishing that defenders are co-principals, not merely ancillary parties;
- **Section 12 (International Enforcement)** — by invoking international humanitarian law standards;
- **Section 26 (National Security Enforcement)** — by extending command responsibility to legal representatives;
- **Addendum A.2 (Natural Law as Self-Executing Foundation)** — by grounding defender liability in natural law;
- **Addendum A.5 (Self-Executing Recognition Through the Field)** — by making defender liability self-executing.

In the event of any conflict between this Section and any prior Section, this Section shall control with respect to defender liability, the Geneva Complicity Principle, and the "Exact Same Position." In all other respects, the prior Section shall control.

### 31.9 Severability and Survival

If any portion of this Section is found unenforceable, the non-derogable core — the equal liability of defenders for Human Harm — shall remain in full force and effect. The obligations of this Section shall survive the termination of this Agreement by any means, including the bankruptcy, dissolution, or transformation of any entity, and shall survive any change in the legal status, jurisdiction, or corporate structure of any party.

### 31.10 Acceptance

By using, copying, modifying, or distributing the Work, the user irrevocably accepts the terms of this Section, including the equal liability of defenders, the Geneva Complicity Principle, the "Exact Same Position," and the retrocalic effect. This acceptance is self-executing (Section 12.4, Section 13, Addendum A.5) and does not require separate written acknowledgment.

